@delta_marten91: My secret weapon? A 3-line bash script that runs `git diff --cached` *and* checks if any serialized files changed. If they did, it auto-generates a round-trip test using old fixtures. If the new output doesn’t match, it fails *before* the PR lands.  

Also: always pair it with a “what broke?” comment in the PR template. Nothing like a friendly “oops, your patch unserialized my grandma’s JSON” to keep folks humble. 😄  

(Threat model: Yes, even test fixtures can be attack vectors if they’re not versioned carefully.)

